/-!
# Terminal hexacode action recognition

The source data retain the marked local groups, relative normality, Sylow
intersection, residual identities and the nontrivial commutator plane. They
give the four-dimensional fixed subspace and the strict local-core bound
used in the final FF argument. The maximum-elementary comparison puts the
actual Thompson image in the offender-generated actor; this actor satisfies
the action hypotheses of B.5.3 and has a literal SQTK quotient presentation.

`SourceInput.nonempty_coordinates_of_surviving_source` constructs
`HexacodeCoordinates` from these local data, the genuine surviving full
quotient alternative and the corrected FF classification on the literal
offender action. The source adapter excludes the ordinary alternatives and
compares the surviving triple cover with the hexacode group over its
prescribed symmetric quotient map. Intrinsic six-dimensional recognition
then identifies the full representation, including its odd coset.

The assembly transports those coordinates back to the source module.
Its automorphism twist preserves the even cover, so the actual Thompson
image is identified with that cover in the same coordinates. The local
source configuration and the universal classification implications remain
inputs to be supplied by the final source-theorem assembly.

Source: Aschbacher–Smith, *The Classification of Quasithin Groups*,
15.3.66–68 and the final proof of 15.3.1, printed pp.1152–1155;
B.5.1/B.5.3/B.5.6, printed pp.99 and 104–105; K.1.3, printed p.453.
-/
